1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,968
Suppose Aziz is at the MyID step, on the way to his first transfer.

2
00:00:05,018 --> 00:00:08,976
The mobile bank asks heeyID to open his MyID check.

3
00:00:09,026 --> 00:00:13,608
At that minute, MyID starts to answer slowly, and then not at all.

4
00:00:13,658 --> 00:00:15,480
Aziz looks at a spinner.

5
00:00:15,530 --> 00:00:20,168
What happens to him, and what happens to the platform?

6
00:00:20,218 --> 00:00:27,752
Lesson 3 named the services outside the bank, and Lesson 4 gave the configuration of the installation.

7
00:00:27,802 --> 00:00:33,104
Here you see how the platform talks to those services, and what it does when one of them stops answering.

8
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:35,984
Start in the middle, with the core of heeyID.

9
00:00:36,034 --> 00:00:41,096
The core holds the rules: registration, proofing, devices and login.

10
00:00:41,146 --> 00:00:44,144
The core itself has no input and no output.

11
00:00:44,194 --> 00:00:46,472
It never opens a connection of its own.

12
00:00:46,522 --> 00:00:50,408
When it needs something from outside, it never calls the service directly.

13
00:00:50,458 --> 00:00:55,688
It asks in its own words: open a proofing session, send a code, send a push.

14
00:00:55,738 --> 00:01:01,112
Each request goes to an adapter: a small program that talks to one real service.

15
00:01:01,162 --> 00:01:08,600
heeyID has four of them for the outside world: MyID, TAF, SMS and push.

16
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:13,616
The TAF adapter calls the TAF agent inside the bank network.

17
00:01:13,666 --> 00:01:16,208
The other three go out through the egress proxy.

18
00:01:16,258 --> 00:01:22,472
Each adapter runs as a unit of its own, and only the customer gateway and the workers call it.

19
00:01:22,522 --> 00:01:24,344
Now the tests.

20
00:01:24,394 --> 00:01:29,096
Each outside service has one real adapter, and no second version for the tests.

21
00:01:29,146 --> 00:01:33,920
For the tests, a simulator takes the place of the outside service itself.

22
00:01:33,970 --> 00:01:41,528
It speaks the same API as MyID, TAF, the SMS vendor or the push service.

23
00:01:41,578 --> 00:01:48,656
It gives every answer that the contract allows: success, each failure reason, a timeout and a service that is down.

24
00:01:48,706 --> 00:01:53,384
So the real adapter runs in every acceptance test, with no internet.

25
00:01:53,434 --> 00:01:57,416
Each night, one more run goes against the MyID test environment.

26
00:01:57,466 --> 00:02:02,024
A team that tests only against the real service tests only the good days.

27
00:02:02,074 --> 00:02:05,336
The simulator brings the bad days into every run.

28
00:02:05,386 --> 00:02:07,208
Now the walls around each call.

29
00:02:07,258 --> 00:02:11,552
Each outgoing call has a timeout: how long the adapter waits for an answer.

30
00:02:11,602 --> 00:02:13,688
Each adapter also has a breaker.

31
00:02:13,738 --> 00:02:17,744
After a configured number of failures in a row, the breaker opens.

32
00:02:17,794 --> 00:02:21,368
While it is open, calls fail at once, with no waiting.

33
00:02:21,418 --> 00:02:27,560
A test call goes to the service at a configured interval, and the breaker closes when the service answers again.

34
00:02:27,610 --> 00:02:32,720
While the breaker is open, operations get an alert: a dependency is down.

35
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:38,960
The timeouts and the breaker values are installation settings, like the MyID host in Lesson 4.

36
00:02:39,010 --> 00:02:42,776
The panel cannot change them, and a change needs a new deployment.

37
00:02:42,826 --> 00:02:48,056
And now the most important rule: each outside service has one fixed behaviour when it is down.

38
00:02:48,106 --> 00:02:52,112
The adapter makes that behaviour impossible to bypass.

39
00:02:52,162 --> 00:02:58,424
A timeout always gives one plain answer, never a raw error that each caller reads in its own way.

40
00:02:58,474 --> 00:03:03,752
MyID is down: the customer sees that the service is temporarily not available.

41
00:03:03,802 --> 00:03:08,624
The customer stays at the current level, and the attempt does not count against the limits.

42
00:03:08,674 --> 00:03:12,680
Nothing runs later in the background, so the customer starts the check again.

43
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:19,064
TAF is down: the code goes through the SMS service of the bank, and the attempt is not scored.

44
00:03:19,114 --> 00:03:27,824
One refusal is not an outage: when the state services portal names another bank as the trusted bank, no code goes by SMS.

45
00:03:27,874 --> 00:03:33,632
The SMS vendor is down: a code that needs an SMS cannot go now.

46
00:03:33,682 --> 00:03:37,472
The notifications wait in Kafka, and go again later.

47
00:03:37,522 --> 00:03:42,200
The push service is down: the notifications go by SMS only.

48
00:03:42,250 --> 00:03:44,456
Back to Aziz and his spinner.

49
00:03:44,506 --> 00:03:50,528
The MyID adapter waits for its timeout, then gives its plain answer: the provider is not available.

50
00:03:50,578 --> 00:03:54,296
Aziz sees a short message, and his level does not move.

51
00:03:54,346 --> 00:03:56,168
His attempt does not count.

52
00:03:56,218 --> 00:04:00,248
MyID fails for the next customers too, so the breaker opens.

53
00:04:00,298 --> 00:04:03,656
Now they get the same answer at once, with no spinner.

54
00:04:03,706 --> 00:04:09,440
When MyID answers the test call again, the breaker closes, and Aziz starts his check again.

55
00:04:09,490 --> 00:04:11,960
Two more rules keep the adapters safe.

56
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:16,688
First, no adapter writes a request body or a response body to a log.

57
00:04:16,738 --> 00:04:20,648
It writes the reference, the outcome and the time that the call took.

58
00:04:20,698 --> 00:04:27,104
Second, hosts and credentials come only from the installation configuration and Vault, never from the code.

59
00:04:27,154 --> 00:04:29,936
Each adapter holds only its own credential.

60
00:04:29,986 --> 00:04:37,864
Now a check: TAF stops answering while a customer registers, so how does the code reach him?

61
00:04:37,914 --> 00:04:42,952
Through the SMS service of the bank, and that registration is not scored.

62
00:04:43,002 --> 00:04:46,408
The core of heeyID has no input and no output.

63
00:04:46,458 --> 00:04:50,632
Four adapters, and a simulator for each outside service.

64
00:04:50,682 --> 00:04:55,600
A timeout and a breaker on every call, and one fixed behaviour for each outage.

65
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:58,912
So Aziz waits a few seconds, not forever.

66
00:04:58,962 --> 00:05:08,464
Each adapter holds one secret: the MyID credential, the TAF client secret, the SMS vendor login and the push credential.

67
00:05:08,514 --> 00:05:12,376
Lesson 6 shows where those keys live, and who holds them.
